# Copy to .env and fill in. Validated at boot by src/lib/env.ts.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/subiq"
# Direct (non-pooled) connection used ONLY by Prisma CLI/migrations.
# In production: DATABASE_URL = POOLED connection (serverless runtime),
# DIRECT_URL = direct connection (e.g. Supabase port 5432). Locally leave unset
# to reuse DATABASE_URL. Read by prisma.config.ts, not the app runtime.
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/subiq"
# Auth.js secret (openssl rand -base64 33) — required in production
AUTH_SECRET=""
# Bearer token for /api/cron/* (openssl rand -hex 24) — required in
# production; unset in dev, cron routes are open on localhost.
CRON_SECRET=""

# Optional: real email sending in production
# RESEND_API_KEY=""
# EMAIL_FROM="SubIQ <login@yourdomain.com>"

# Optional OAuth (set both halves of a pair)
# AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
# AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""
# AUTH_GITHUB_ID=""
# AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=""


⏺ The key thing: production doesn't use a .env file

  Your .env file is local-only — it's gitignored and only used when you run pnpm
  dev on your machine. It never goes to production.

  In production, Vercel injects environment variables that you set in its
  dashboard: Project → Settings → Environment Variables. That's "the env file"
  for prod. So your question is really "which variables do I set in Vercel?" —
  here they are.

  What to set in Vercel (Production scope)

  Required — the app won't boot without these (enforced by src/lib/env.ts):

  Variable: DATABASE_URL
  Value: Supabase transaction pooler, port :6543, add ?pgbouncer=true
  Where it comes from: Supabase → Settings → Database → Connection string →
  Transaction pooler
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Variable: DIRECT_URL
  Value: Supabase session/direct, port :5432
  Where it comes from: Same page → Session pooler (this is basically what your
  local .env already has)
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Variable: AUTH_SECRET
  Value: f9Ld03vWrpBnQmxFENjW3HdS4wR7q7gd1Z+fr1lQQs+n
  Where it comes from: ← just generated for you
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Variable: CRON_SECRET
  Value: cd264dadc9862c4fb5fa7fbd97afa0604c29404626b56c7e
  Where it comes from: ← just generated for you

  Effectively required — magic-link login is dead without email:

  ┌────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │    Variable    │                          Value                          │
  ├────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ RESEND_API_KEY │ Your Resend API key (from resend.com dashboard)         │
  ├────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ EMAIL_FROM     │ SubIQ <login@yourdomain.com> — must be a verified       │
  │                │ domain in Resend                                        │
  └────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Optional — only if you want social login: AUTH_GOOGLE_ID + AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET,
  and/or AUTH_GITHUB_ID + AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET (set both halves of a pair or
  neither).

  Do NOT set NODE_ENV — Vercel sets it to production automatically.

  Why two database URLs (the part that's probably confusing)

  - DATABASE_URL = the pooled one (:6543). Your live app runs on serverless
  functions; without pooling they'd exhaust Postgres connections.
  - DIRECT_URL = the direct one (:5432). Only prisma migrate deploy uses it
  (during the Vercel build), because migrations need a real direct connection
  that a transaction pooler can't provide.

  Your local .env stays exactly as it is — a single DATABASE_URL is fine for dev
  (the migration config falls back to it when DIRECT_URL is unset). You only
  add the two-URL split in Vercel.

  Concretely, from what you already have

  Your current .env DATABASE_URL is ...pooler.supabase.com:5432 (the session
  pooler). So in Vercel:
  - DIRECT_URL = that same :5432 string you already have.
  - DATABASE_URL = the same string but swap the port to :6543 and append
  ?pgbouncer=true (grab the exact "Transaction pooler" string from Supabase to
  be safe).

  One thing to watch at your first deploy (step 5 smoke test): if the runtime
  throws prepared-statement errors against the :6543 pooler, that's the known
  pooler-compatibility check — easily fixed, and I flagged it for that stage.

  Want me to add a # --- Production (set these in Vercel, not here) ---
  reference block to .env.example so this list lives in the repo next to the
  local vars?